  "title": "13.EXCHANGE RATE & TRADE",
  "order": 233,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "fp0yzff",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. EXCHANGE RATE DETERMINATION (DEMAND–SUPPLY THEORY)"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Exchange rates are primarily determined by the market forces of demand and supply in the foreign exchange market.",
        "Rupee depreciation makes exports cheaper for foreign buyers and imports more expensive for domestic consumers.",
        "Rupee appreciation makes imports cheaper while making domestic exports less cost-competitive abroad.",
        "Analyzing India's major trade partners and export-import composition is essential for understanding the country's balance of trade.",
        "RRB high-value exam questions frequently test the economic effects of currency value fluctuations on trade deficits."
      ],
      "id": "5aoe7h1"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Determination Theory",
          "value": "Demand–Supply",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Currency Dynamics",
          "value": "Depreciation vs Appreciation",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Core Focus",
          "value": "Trade Partners & Composition",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Target Exam",
          "value": "RRB & Competitive",
          "icon": "info"
        }
      ],
      "id": "8cjof5o"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jj891yy",
      "content": "Under a flexible exchange rate system, the value of a currency is determined by the demand and supply of foreign exchange in the forex market."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sq09vc8",
      "content": "A. Demand for Foreign Exchange arises due to:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hl0exr8",
      "content": "• Imports of goods and services<br></br>• Foreign travel, education and medical expenses<br></br>• Interest and dividend payments abroad<br></br>• Capital outflows and investment abroad"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h5p69mg",
      "content": "B. Supply of Foreign Exchange arises due to:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xit67lu",
      "content": "• Exports of goods and services<br></br>• Remittances from NRIs<br></br>• Foreign Direct Investment (FDI)<br></br>• Foreign Portfolio Investment (FPI)<br></br>• External borrowings"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vk1yak8",
      "content": "Equilibrium exchange rate is determined at the intersection point of demand and supply curves. If demand for dollars increases, rupee depreciates. If supply increases, rupee appreciates."
    },
    {
